    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2040047247703130191",
      "url": "https://x.com/laiteranidaddy/status/2040047247703130191",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/laiteranidaddy/status/2040047247703130191",
      "text": "👑 𝕾𝖎𝖈𝖎𝖑𝖑𝖎𝖆𝖓 𝕲𝖆𝖒𝖇𝖎𝖙 👑\n       𝐓𝐇𝐄 𝐌𝐀𝐑𝐂𝐎𝐒\n𝐆𝐑𝐀𝐍𝐃 𝐁𝐋𝐔𝐄𝐏𝐑𝐈𝐍𝐓\n         🅂🄴🅁🄸🄴🅂\n\nSeason 1 \n𝑻𝒉𝒆 𝑷𝒐𝒘𝒆𝒓 𝑫𝒐𝒄𝒕𝒓𝒊𝒏𝒆\nEpisode 3\n\"𝑻𝒉𝒆 𝑴𝒐𝒕𝒉𝒃𝒂𝒍𝒍 𝑶𝒓𝒅𝒆𝒓\"\n\n     \"𝑻𝒉𝒆𝒚 𝒄𝒂𝒍𝒍𝒆𝒅 𝒊𝒕 𝒔𝒂𝒇𝒆𝒕𝒚.\n     𝑻𝒉𝒆 𝒆𝒏𝒈𝒊𝒏𝒆𝒆𝒓𝒔 𝒄𝒂𝒍𝒍𝒆𝒅  \n     𝒊𝒕 𝒔𝒄𝒊𝒆𝒏𝒄𝒆. 𝑻𝒉𝒆 \n     𝒏𝒖𝒎𝒃𝒆𝒓 𝒄𝒂𝒍𝒍 𝒊𝒕 𝒂 \n     ⁹𝒎𝒊𝒔𝒕𝒂𝒌𝒆.\"\n\n𝑴𝒈𝒂 𝑲𝒂-𝑺𝒂𝒌𝒂𝒚,\nOctober 1, 1986.\nSix months after EDSA. \n\nThe BNPP — built, completed, and ready — received its death sentence in six words:\n\n\"𝙁𝙤𝙧 𝙧𝙚𝙖𝙨𝙤𝙣𝙨 𝙤𝙛 𝙨𝙖𝙛𝙚𝙩𝙮 𝙖𝙣𝙙 𝙚𝙘𝙤𝙣𝙤𝙢𝙮.\"\n\nThat is the entire justification in \n𝙀𝙭𝙚𝙘𝙪𝙩𝙞𝙫𝙚 𝙊𝙧𝙙𝙚𝙧 𝙉𝙤. 55 for one of the most consequential decisions in the history of the \n𝑳𝒖𝒑𝒂𝒏𝒈 𝑻𝒊𝒏𝒖𝒃𝒖𝒂𝒏.\n\n🔹️ The Chernobyl Comparison — \nA Scientific Mismatch\n\nThe timing of EO 55 was not coincidental. \n\nAquino's order came months after the Chernobyl nuclear disaster in the Soviet Union.\n\nHere is what the critics never tell you:\n\n𝘾𝙝𝙚𝙧𝙣𝙤𝙗𝙮𝙡 𝙬𝙖𝙨 𝙖 𝙎𝙤𝙫𝙞𝙚𝙩 𝙍𝘽𝙈𝙆 𝙧𝙚𝙖𝙘𝙩𝙤𝙧  -𝙜𝙧𝙖𝙥𝙝𝙞𝙩𝙚-𝙢𝙤𝙙𝙚𝙧𝙖𝙩𝙙 𝙙𝙚𝙨𝙞𝙜𝙣 𝙬𝙞𝙩𝙝 𝙣𝙤 𝙘𝙤𝙣𝙩𝙖𝙞𝙣𝙢𝙚𝙣𝙩 𝙨𝙩𝙧𝙪𝙘𝙩𝙪𝙧𝙚\n\nNone. \n\nThe BNPP was a Westinghouse pressurized water reactor — the same class of technology used safely across the United States, France, Japan, and South Korea.\n\nComparing Chernobyl to the BNPP is the scientific equivalent of banning all aircraft because the Hindenburg caught fire. \n\nThey are not the same technology. \n\nThey are not the same risk.\n\n🔹️ What the DOST Said — The Government's Own Scientists\n\nDr. Carlo Arcilla, Director of the Philippine Nuclear Research Institute under the Department of Science and Technology, stated plainly that the two reasons cited for mothballing the BNPP — a nearby volcano and an earthquake fault line — were both \"𝙛𝙖𝙠𝙚 𝙣𝙚𝙬𝙨.\"\n\nHe noted that Laguna de Bay is also a volcano — younger than Mount Natib — and asked: \n\n\"Are you afraid of Laguna de Bay?\"\n\nThe BNPP was built to withstand a 0.4G seismic shield and can cope with the crash of a 747 airplane.\n\nThe safety arguments were not science. \n\nThey were politics wearing a lab coat.\n\n🔹️ The Hawaii Conversation — February 1989\n\nThree years after \nEO 55 — with the Philippines already drowning in 8-hour blackouts — a dying 𝑭𝒆𝒓𝒅𝒊𝒏𝒂𝒏𝒅 𝑴𝒂𝒓𝒄𝒐𝒔 lay in the ICU of St. Francis Hospital in Honolulu.\n\nFormer Vice President Salvador Laurel wrote in his 1998 Manila Bulletin column that on \nFebruary 3, 1989, he visited Marcos in Hawaii. \nMarcos told him:\n\n\"𝑻𝒆𝒍𝒍 𝒉𝒆𝒓 — 𝑰 𝒂𝒎 𝒘𝒊𝒍𝒍𝒊𝒏𝒈 𝒕𝒐 𝒕𝒖𝒓𝒏 𝒐𝒗𝒆𝒓 90% 𝒐𝒇 𝒂𝒍𝒍 𝒎𝒚 𝒆𝒂𝒓𝒕𝒉𝒍𝒚 𝒑𝒐𝒔𝒔𝒆𝒔𝒔𝒊𝒐𝒏𝒔 𝒕𝒐 𝒕𝒉𝒆 𝑭𝒊𝒍𝒊𝒑𝒊𝒏𝒐 𝒑𝒆𝒐𝒑𝒍𝒆. 𝑶𝒏𝒍𝒚 10% 𝒘𝒊𝒍𝒍 𝒈𝒐 𝒕𝒐 𝒎𝒚 𝒇𝒂𝒎𝒊𝒍𝒚. .\n\n𝑨𝒍𝒍 𝑰 𝒂𝒔𝒌 𝒊𝒔 𝒕𝒉𝒂𝒕 𝑰 𝒃𝒆 𝒂𝒍𝒍𝒐𝒘𝒆𝒅 𝒕𝒐 𝒅𝒊𝒆 𝒊𝒏 𝒎𝒚 𝒐𝒘𝒏 𝒄𝒐𝒖𝒏𝒕𝒓𝒚.\"\n\nLaurel returned to Manila on February 4 and immediately sought an appointment with President Aquino to relay the message.\n\nPresident Aquino refused to meet him.\n\nA dying man. \nA peace offer. \n\nA Vice President willing to broker it.\n\nRefused. \nWithout a meeting.\n\n🔹️ Price of That Refusal\n\nWhile the Philippines paid its BNPP debt year after year — the nation paid another price too.\n\n𝙁𝙧𝙤𝙢 1989 𝙩𝙤 1993, 𝙩𝙝𝙚 𝙋𝙝𝙞𝙡𝙞𝙥𝙥𝙞𝙣𝙚𝙨 𝙚𝙭𝙥𝙚𝙧𝙞𝙚𝙣𝙘𝙚𝙙 8 𝙩𝙤 12-𝙝𝙤𝙪𝙧 𝙧𝙤𝙡𝙡𝙞𝙣𝙜 𝙗𝙡𝙖𝙘𝙠𝙤𝙪𝙩𝙨 𝙙𝙖𝙞𝙡𝙮.\n\nBusinesses collapsed. Foreign investments fled. \n\nThe decade that should have been the Philippines' economic takeoff became its darkest energy chapter.\n\nThe Philippines was the first Southeast Asian country to build a nuclear power facility — in 1976.\n\nSouth Korea started its nuclear program at roughly the same time.\n\nToday South Korea has 25 active reactors — providing one-third of the peninsula's electricity. South Korean engineers now build nuclear plants for other countries.",
